A Lisp, and the one thing a reference count cannot do
=====================================================

A Lisp small enough to read in one sitting: seven kinds of value, three special forms, nine builtins,
and a reader that knows integers, symbols and parentheses. It is not here to be a Lisp. It is here
because a Lisp cannot avoid the one shape reference counting cannot reclaim.

The axis this owns is **the reference cycle**, and with it the reason `weak T` is in the language at
all. Nothing else in `guide/` reaches it: `json` is a tree, `scheduler`'s graphs are acyclic in the
direction that matters, and `kernel` avoids the question by giving objects indices instead of
references. A closure has to see the environment it was written in, and a recursive definition puts
that closure *into* that environment, so `(define (fact n) ... (fact ...))` closes a loop on the
first useful thing anyone types.

The interpreter is therefore two interpreters wearing one body. `Lambda` carries the edge back to its
environment in two spellings — `held`, an ordinary `&Env`, and `seen`, a `weak Env` — and an `Interp`
is built one way or the other. Both run the same source to the same answers. What differs is only
what is left over afterwards, which is what this program measures.

**How it measures it is the part worth stealing.** A weak reference does not keep its referent alive,
so a `Buf[weak Env]` holding one witness per environment ever created is a live-object counter that
costs nothing and needs no hook in the runtime. Ask the witnesses after the interpreter is gone, and
the answer is the number of environments that outlived it.

A destructor — `impl Drop for Env`, `03 § A destructor` — would answer the same question by being
told rather than by asking, and it is deliberately not what this uses: it would put the measurement
inside `Env`, and the subject here is what happens to an environment that carries nothing on account
of being measured.

Deliberately not modelled: strings, `quote`, floats, `let`, varargs, macros, error values, and a
garbage collector. The last one is the point rather than an omission — collecting the leak would be
deleting the subject.


What writing it found
---------------------

**What leaks is one environment per environment a definition lands in.** Not one per program, and not
one per call. Writing $D$ for the definitions a run evaluates and $F(d)$ for the set of frames the
definition $d$ was evaluated in, the environments left behind are

$$L = \left| \bigcup_{d \in D} F(d) \right|$$

and everything surprising about the numbers falls out of that union. A *top-level* `define` always
lands in the one globals frame, so every $F(d)$ is the same singleton however many times the function
is later called, and $L = 1$ no matter how long the run is. Move the definition inside a function and
the identical formula reads the other way: each call is its own frame, the union grows with the calls,
and nothing is reclaimed at all.

| program | environments made | $L$ |
|---|---|---|
| one definition, eleven calls | 12 | 1 |
| two definitions, five hundred more calls | 513 | 1 |
| a definition **inside** a function, called three times | 4 | 4 |

The first two rows were the prediction. The third was not — it came from asking rather than from the
design, and it is the row that decides whether a naive interpreter is usable. That is the argument
for having a count rather than an opinion.

**The cure costs more than the disease.** Making the back-edge `weak` means something else has to own
every environment, and the only owner available is the interpreter — so nothing is freed until the
*whole run* is over. The naive interpreter holds one environment forever; the careful one holds every
environment until it exits. ARC offers a choice between two leaks here, and there is no third option,
because the cycle is the semantics rather than an artefact of how the semantics were encoded.

**Breaking a cycle turns a total operation into a partial one.** `held` is an `&Env` and always
answers; `seen` is a `weak Env` and answers `Option`. The interpreter unwraps it on a path that
cannot fail — the owner table is what makes it so — and the unwrap is still written. That is the
honest price of `weak`, and it shows up as code rather than as a comment.

**`eval` and `apply` are mutually recursive, and that is the one kind of tail call sysl does not turn
into a jump.** `12 §3` gives the reason, which is the calling convention rather than a gap in the
walk. So `eval` carries the loop itself: a tail position reassigns `expr` and `env` and goes round
again where a recursive call would have gone.

**The chapter's claim is about sysl and turned out not to be a claim about the stack**, which is worth
writing down because it nearly cost this loop. Built at the default `-O1`, a mutual recursion ten
million deep returns an answer — LLVM's sibling-call pass does what sysl declined to. Built
`--optimize 0`, the identical source segfaults. The loop stays either way: what rescued it is a
back-end pass rather than a guarantee, and the first argument too large for a register would take it
away again. `12 §3` now says so; it did not before this program was written.

**A hundred thousand cons cells come apart without a stack.** `03`'s teardown is iterative, so
dropping a long list drains a worklist rather than recursing. Confirmed here at a scale a recursive
release would not survive — and the list is summed as well as counted, because a count only says how
many cells were reachable while

$$\sum_{k=1}^{100000} k = \frac{100000 \cdot 100001}{2} = 5000050000$$

says that each one still held what was put in it. A teardown bug that freed a cell early would be far
likelier to corrupt a value than to shorten the list.

**Three recursive walks, and one limit closes all of them.** `read_value`, `render` and the non-tail
arms of `eval` each recurse on nesting, and nothing but the reader ever *makes* a nested value —
`cons` grows a list to the right, which every walk over it takes as a loop. So the depth cap belongs
in the reader and belongs nowhere else, and a form the reader accepted is one the other two are
already bounded on. That is a property of the value representation rather than of the code, and it is
the reason the cap is one constant instead of three.

    import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}


The values
----------

Seven of them, and the whole of what a program here can hold. `Pair` is the one that reaches itself,
through `&Value` on both sides — the same indirection `guide/json` needed for a tree, doing something
a tree cannot.

    enum Value
        Nil
        Int(n: long)
        Sym(name: string)
        Bool(b: bool)
        Pair(head: &Value, tail: &Value)
        Closure(lam: &Lambda)
        Builtin(code: int, name: string)
    end Value

A lambda and the environment it was written in. **The two spellings of that one edge are the whole
subject of this program**, and exactly one of them is ever filled: `held` owns what it points at and
makes the cycle, `seen` does not own it and needs somebody else to.

    struct Lambda
        params: &Buf[string]
        body: &Value
        held: Option[&Env]
        seen: weak Env
    end Lambda

A frame of bindings and the frame it was written inside. Names and values are kept in step in two
buffers rather than in one buffer of pairs, because a binding is replaced in place by `define` and
two `set` calls say that more plainly than rebuilding a struct.

    struct Env
        names: &Buf[string]
        slots: &Buf[&Value]
        parent: Option[&Env]
    end Env

`owning` picks which spelling of the closure edge is used, and with it who owns an environment.
`owned` is empty unless it does; `witnesses` is filled either way, and holds nothing alive.

    struct Interp
        globals: &Env
        owned: &Buf[&Env]
        witnesses: &Buf[weak Env]
        owning: bool
    end Interp


Reading text
------------

    enum ReadError
        Expected(at: usize, what: string)
        Unclosed(at: usize)
        Unbalanced(at: usize)
        TooBig(at: usize)
        TooDeep(at: usize)
    end ReadError

    describe(e: ReadError) -> string
        e match
            Expected(at, what) -> s"$what expected at $at"
            Unclosed(at) -> s"unclosed list at $at"
            Unbalanced(at) -> s"unbalanced ')' at $at"
            TooBig(at) -> s"number too large at $at"
            TooDeep(at) -> s"nested too deeply at $at"
    end describe

**One limit, and it bounds every recursion in the interpreter.** The reader is the only thing that
builds a nested value — `cons` grows a list to the right, which is a loop everywhere it is walked —
so a form no deeper than this is one `render` cannot recurse off the stack, and one whose non-tail
`eval` positions are bounded too. Capping the reader is therefore not a limit on input so much as the
single place the three walks are made finite.

    private const max_depth: int = 128

The reader is a position in a source text, and keeps both spellings of it for the same reason
`guide/json` does: the bytes are what decisions are made on, and the `string` is what a symbol's name
is cut out of.

    private struct Reader
        text: string
        src: []const u8
        at: usize

        done(*self) -> bool = self.at >= self.src.len

        bump(*self)
            self.at += 1

        skip_ws(*self)
            while !self.done()
                var c = self.src[self.at]

                if c == 32 || c == 9 || c == 10 || c == 13 then self.bump()
                else break
        end skip_ws

A symbol runs to the next space or paren, which is the whole of the token grammar: there are no
strings, no quote and no comments, so nothing else can end one.

        delimiter(*self, c: u8) -> bool =
            c == 32 || c == 9 || c == 10 || c == 13 || c == u8('(') || c == u8(')')

`-` is the subtraction builtin when nothing follows it, and a sign when a digit does. That one lookahead
is the only place the token grammar is not decidable from a single byte.

        read_value(*self, depth: int) -> Result[&Value, ReadError]
            if depth > max_depth then return Err(TooDeep(self.at))
            self.skip_ws()
            if self.done() then return Err(Expected(self.at, "a value"))

            var c = self.src[self.at]

            if c == u8(')') then return Err(Unbalanced(self.at))
            if c == u8('(') then return self.read_list(depth)

            if digit(c) || (c == u8('-') && self.at + 1 < self.src.len && digit(self.src[self.at + 1])) then
                return self.read_number()

            self.read_symbol()
        end read_value

The elements are gathered and the list is built from the tail back, because a list is a right-nested
pair and there is nothing to hang the front of one on until the end is known.

        read_list(*self, depth: int) -> Result[&Value, ReadError]
            var open = self.at

            self.bump()

            var items: &Buf[&Value] = buf()

            while true
                self.skip_ws()
                if self.done() then return Err(Unclosed(open))
                if self.src[self.at] == u8(')')
                    self.bump()
                    break

                items.push(self.read_value(depth + 1)?)

            var out: &Value = Nil
            var i = items.len()

            while i > 0
                i -= 1
                out = Pair(items.at(i), out)

            Ok(out)
        end read_list

`12x` is one token, so a digit run that stops at something other than a delimiter is a malformed
number rather than a number followed by a symbol.

        read_number(*self) -> Result[&Value, ReadError]
            var start = self.at
            var neg = self.src[self.at] == u8('-')

            if neg then self.bump()

            var n: long = 0
            var digits = 0

            while !self.done() && digit(self.src[self.at])
                n = n * 10 + long(int(self.src[self.at]) - 48)
                digits += 1
                if digits > 18 then return Err(TooBig(start))
                self.bump()

            if !self.done() && !self.delimiter(self.src[self.at]) then
                return Err(Expected(self.at, "a delimiter"))

            Ok(Int(if neg then -n else n))
        end read_number

        read_symbol(*self) -> Result[&Value, ReadError]
            var start = self.at

            while !self.done() && !self.delimiter(self.src[self.at])
                self.bump()

            Ok(Sym(self.text[start..<self.at]))
        end read_symbol
    end Reader

    private digit(c: u8) -> bool = c >= 48 && c <= 57

Every form in a source text, in order. A program is a sequence of them and its value is the last
one's, so the reader hands back all of them rather than one.

    read_all(text: string) -> Result[&Buf[&Value], ReadError]
        var r = Reader(text, text.bytes, 0)
        var forms: &Buf[&Value] = buf()

        while true
            r.skip_ws()
            if r.done() then break
            forms.push(r.read_value(0)?)

        Ok(forms)
    end read_all

Read one form and write it straight back out, evaluating nothing. A form that is well-formed text and
not a well-formed *program* — a list whose head is an integer, a nesting no evaluation would survive
— can be checked this way and no other, since evaluating it is what would stop the run.

    reads_back(src: string) -> string
        read_all(src) match
            Ok(forms) -> if forms.len() == 1 then render(forms.at(0)) else s"${forms.len()} forms"
            Err(e) -> s"error: ${describe(e)}"
    end reads_back


Writing it back
---------------

    render(v: &Value) -> string
        *v match
            Nil -> "()"
            Int(n) -> str(n)
            Sym(name) -> name
            Bool(b) -> if b then "true" else "false"
            Builtin(_, name) -> s"#<$name>"
            Closure(_) -> "#<lambda>"
            Pair(_, _) -> render_list(v)
    end render

A pair whose tail is not a list is written the way it was built, so nothing the interpreter can make
is unprintable. Only `cons` produces one; the reader cannot.

    private render_list(v: &Value) -> string
        var out = "("
        var cur = v
        var first = true

        while true
            *cur match
                Pair(head, tail) ->
                    if !first then out = out + " "
                    out = out + render(head)
                    first = false
                    cur = tail
                Nil -> break
                _ ->
                    out = out + " . " + render(cur)
                    break

        out + ")"
    end render_list


Environments
------------

Every environment ever made passes through here, which is what makes the witness list complete — and
a witness list with a hole in it would understate a leak rather than fail.

    private new_env(i: &Interp, parent: Option[&Env]) -> &Env
        var e: &Env = Env(buf(), buf(), parent)

        i.witnesses.push(e)
        if i.owning then i.owned.push(e)

        e
    end new_env

`define` on a name already bound in this frame replaces it, which is what makes a definition
re-readable and what lets a program be given twice.

    private define_in(e: &Env, name: string, v: &Value)
        for i in 0..<e.names.len()
            if e.names.at(i) == name
                e.slots.set(i, v)
                return

        e.names.push(name)
        e.slots.push(v)
    end define_in

    private lookup(env: &Env, name: string) -> &Value
        var cur: Option[&Env] = Some(env)

        while cur.is_some()
            var e = cur.unwrap()

            for i in 0..<e.names.len()
                if e.names.at(i) == name then return e.slots.at(i)

            cur = e.parent

        panic(s"unbound name '$name'")
    end lookup

The one place the two spellings of the closure edge are read, and **the price of the weak one**: an
`Option` on a path the owner table has already made total. The unwrap can never fail and is written
anyway, which is what breaking a cycle costs in a language that will not let a reference dangle.

    private env_of(lam: &Lambda) -> &Env
        lam.held match
            Some(e) -> e
            None ->
                lam.seen.get() match
                    Some(e) -> e
                    None -> panic("a closure outlived the environment it was written in")
    end env_of

    private make_closure(i: &Interp, params: &Buf[string], body: &Value, env: &Env) -> &Value
        if i.owning then Closure(Lambda(params, body, None, env))
        else Closure(Lambda(params, body, Some(env), None))
    end make_closure


Evaluating
----------

    private car(v: &Value) -> &Value
        *v match
            Pair(head, _) -> head
            _ -> panic(s"'${render(v)}' is not a pair")
    end car

    private cdr(v: &Value) -> &Value
        *v match
            Pair(_, tail) -> tail
            _ -> panic(s"'${render(v)}' is not a pair")
    end cdr

    private nth(v: &Value, n: int) -> &Value
        var cur = v

        for _ in 0..<n do cur = cdr(cur)

        car(cur)
    end nth

    private list_len(v: &Value) -> int
        var cur = v
        var n = 0

        while true
            *cur match
                Pair(_, tail) ->
                    n += 1
                    cur = tail
                _ -> break

        n
    end list_len

The symbol at the head of a form, or the empty string for anything else. A form headed by something
that is not a symbol is an application, and so is one headed by a symbol that is not a special form —
the two cases meet again below.

    private head_symbol(v: &Value) -> string
        *v match
            Sym(name) -> name
            _ -> ""
    end head_symbol

Only `false` is false. Every other value, `()` included, is true — the Scheme rule rather than the
Common Lisp one, because `null?` already answers the other question and one of the two has to go.

    private truthy(v: &Value) -> bool
        *v match
            Bool(b) -> b
            _ -> true
    end truthy

    private param_names(v: &Value) -> &Buf[string]
        var names: &Buf[string] = buf()
        var cur = v

        while true
            *cur match
                Pair(head, tail) ->
                    *head match
                        Sym(name) -> names.push(name)
                        _ -> panic(s"'${render(head)}' is not a parameter name")
                    cur = tail
                Nil -> break
                _ -> panic("a parameter list is a list")

        names
    end param_names

**`eval` carries its own loop.** A tail position reassigns `expr` and `env` and goes round again
instead of calling `eval`. Written the obvious way, a Lisp function that tail-calls itself a hundred
thousand times would need a hundred thousand sysl frames, because eval-and-apply is mutual recursion
and `12 §3` does not turn that into a jump; written this way it needs one. The chapter's claim and
what the stack actually does are two different things, which is set out above and cost this loop a
near miss.

    eval(i: &Interp, expr0: &Value, env0: &Env) -> &Value
        var expr = expr0
        var env = env0

        while true
            *expr match
                Sym(name) -> return lookup(env, name)
                Pair(head, tail) ->
                    var form = head_symbol(head)

`if` is where the loop earns its keep. The test is evaluated by a real recursive call, because its
value is needed here; the chosen branch is *not* — it is the value of the whole form, so assigning it
to `expr` and falling out of the arm is exactly a tail call, and the frame is reused rather than
stacked.

                    if form == "if"
                        if list_len(tail) != 3 then panic("'if' takes a test and two branches")

                        var test = eval(i, nth(tail, 0), env)

                        expr = if truthy(test) then nth(tail, 1) else nth(tail, 2)

`lambda` and `define` both return rather than looping, and both go through `make_closure` — which is
the single place the cycle is either made or avoided, depending on which interpreter this is.

                    else if form == "lambda"
                        if list_len(tail) != 2 then panic("'lambda' takes a parameter list and a body")

                        return make_closure(i, param_names(nth(tail, 0)), nth(tail, 1), env)
                    else if form == "define"
                        return eval_define(i, tail, env)

Anything else is an application. The head and every argument are evaluated left to right, each by a
recursive call, because every one of their values is needed before the call can happen.

                    else
                        var callee = eval(i, head, env)
                        var args: &Buf[&Value] = buf()
                        var cur = tail

                        while true
                            *cur match
                                Pair(a, rest) ->
                                    args.push(eval(i, a, env))
                                    cur = rest
                                _ -> break

A builtin answers on the spot. A closure does not: **the body is a tail position**, so its frame is
built, the arguments are bound into it, and `expr` and `env` are reassigned to loop again rather than
to recurse. That is the whole reason a Lisp function can tail-call itself a hundred thousand times
here and cost one sysl frame.

`env_of` is where the price of the cure is paid — see above. In the naive interpreter it reads a
field; in the careful one it unwraps an `Option` that cannot be empty, because the interpreter's own
table is holding the environment up.

                        *callee match
                            Builtin(code, name) -> return apply_builtin(code, name, args)
                            Closure(lam) ->
                                var frame = new_env(i, Some(env_of(lam)))

                                if lam.params.len() != args.len() then
                                    panic(s"a lambda of ${lam.params.len()} parameters got ${args.len()} arguments")

                                for k in 0..<lam.params.len()
                                    frame.names.push(lam.params.at(k))
                                    frame.slots.push(args.at(k))

                                expr = lam.body
                                env = frame
                            _ -> panic(s"'${render(callee)}' is not applicable")

An integer, a boolean, `()`, and anything already evaluated stand for themselves.

                _ -> return expr

        expr
    end eval

`(define x e)` and `(define (f a b) e)`, the second being the first with a lambda written for it.
**The shorthand is what closes the cycle**: the closure it makes sees the environment the binding is
about to land in, which is the only way the recursive call could find its own name.

    private eval_define(i: &Interp, tail: &Value, env: &Env) -> &Value
        if list_len(tail) != 2 then panic("'define' takes a target and a value")

        var target = nth(tail, 0)

        *target match
            Sym(name) ->
                define_in(env, name, eval(i, nth(tail, 1), env))
                Sym(name)
            Pair(f, params) ->
                var name = head_symbol(f)

                if name == "" then panic("a definition names a function with a symbol")

                define_in(env, name, make_closure(i, param_names(params), nth(tail, 1), env))
                Sym(name)
            _ -> panic("'define' takes a name or a call shape")
    end eval_define


Builtins
--------

    private const b_add: int = 0
    private const b_sub: int = 1
    private const b_mul: int = 2
    private const b_eq: int = 3
    private const b_lt: int = 4
    private const b_cons: int = 5
    private const b_car: int = 6
    private const b_cdr: int = 7
    private const b_null: int = 8

    private arity(code: int) -> usize = if code == b_car || code == b_cdr || code == b_null then 1 else 2

    private int_of(v: &Value, name: string) -> long
        *v match
            Int(n) -> n
            _ -> panic(s"'$name' wants an integer, got '${render(v)}'")
    end int_of

    private apply_builtin(code: int, name: string, args: &Buf[&Value]) -> &Value
        if args.len() != arity(code) then
            panic(s"'$name' takes ${arity(code)} arguments, got ${args.len()}")

        if code == b_cons then return Pair(args.at(0), args.at(1))
        if code == b_car then return car(args.at(0))
        if code == b_cdr then return cdr(args.at(0))

        if code == b_null
            *args.at(0) match
                Nil -> return Bool(true)
                _ -> return Bool(false)

        var a = int_of(args.at(0), name)
        var b = int_of(args.at(1), name)

        if code == b_add then Int(a + b)
        else if code == b_sub then Int(a - b)
        else if code == b_mul then Int(a * b)
        else if code == b_eq then Bool(a == b)
        else Bool(a < b)
    end apply_builtin


Running one
-----------

An interpreter, and the choice this whole program is about. `witnesses` is handed in from outside
rather than made here, because what it is for is to be read *after* the interpreter is gone — a
counter the interpreter owned would die with the thing it was counting.

    interp(owning: bool, witnesses: &Buf[weak Env]) -> &Interp
        var i: &Interp = Interp(Env(buf(), buf(), None), buf(), witnesses, owning)

        i.globals = new_env(i, None)

        define_in(i.globals, "true", Bool(true))
        define_in(i.globals, "false", Bool(false))
        define_in(i.globals, "+", Builtin(b_add, "+"))
        define_in(i.globals, "-", Builtin(b_sub, "-"))
        define_in(i.globals, "*", Builtin(b_mul, "*"))
        define_in(i.globals, "=", Builtin(b_eq, "="))
        define_in(i.globals, "<", Builtin(b_lt, "<"))
        define_in(i.globals, "cons", Builtin(b_cons, "cons"))
        define_in(i.globals, "car", Builtin(b_car, "car"))
        define_in(i.globals, "cdr", Builtin(b_cdr, "cdr"))
        define_in(i.globals, "null?", Builtin(b_null, "null?"))

        i
    end interp

A program's value is its last form's, and an empty program's is `()`.

    run(i: &Interp, src: string) -> Result[string, ReadError]
        var forms = read_all(src)?
        var out: &Value = Nil

        for k in 0..<forms.len()
            out = eval(i, forms.at(k), i.globals)

        Ok(render(out))
    end run
